    }
    // ...
    fclose(file);
    return 0; // Success
}
```

### Using the `errno` Variable

Another way to return errors in C is by using the `errno` variable, which is a global variable that stores the error code of the last system call that failed. You can set `errno` to a custom value when an error occurs.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <errno.h>

void read_file(const char *filename) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        errno = FILE_READ_ERROR; // Custom error code
        return;
    }
    // ...
    fclose(file);
}
```

### Using the `return` Statement

You can also return an error code directly using the `return` statement. This is a simple and straightforward approach.

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int read_file(const char *filename) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        return FILE_READ_ERROR;
    }
    // ...
    fclose(file);
    return 0; // Success
}
```

### Using the `perror` Function

The `perror` function can be used to print an error message to the standard error stream and return an error code.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

void read_file(const char *filename) {
    FILE *file = fopen(filename, "r");
    if (file == NULL) {
        perror("Error opening file");
        return -1; // Error code
    }
    // ...
    fclose(file);
}
```

These are a few common ways to return errors in C. The choice of method depends on your specific requirements and the type of error you want to return.

